how much should investor get so I got a  great question in front of my YouTube  viewers Sergey love you guys who wrote  in the say I have a difficult question  for me and for many people one person  inventor believed in me and put money in  my business for example $1000 the  question is how do I divide with him the  profits what percentage to give him and  what terms and what time frame possible  and for how long I run a business on  100% he is only the investor thank you  pH would be great to commit a video  response for all how to make a  relationship with investors so Sergey  here is your video now this is a common  question and as entrepreneurs you know  we want to often raise capital if you  can't do it ourselves we need we need  money to launch this thing and make it  big time there are four main ways to  structure a deal with an investor and  each deal is different so there's no  easy way to say hey if it gives you  $1,000 and here's how you want to pay it  back each single deal every deal is  different this is coming from background  and angel and venture capital investing  so give you the four options and you can  see what feels right and how you want to  match it up with your investor the first  is dead so debt is a loan they don't own  any of your business but they're giving  you money that you have to pay back and  to give you $1,000 maybe you say it's a  10 percent interest rate and every year  you have to pay that 10 percent plus  some of the principal down just like  going to a bank and getting a loan but  often the rates are going to be a lot  more favorable towards the investor than  you obviously often you would go when  you can't get a loan from a bank because  it's too risky so you have to pay a  premium but that's death number two is  equity so equity is their taking a piece  of your company so they don't you don't  have to pay them anything back on a  monthly basis or quarterly basis or  whatever it is but they own part of your  company so if they're giving you $1,000  maybe they get 10% of your company now  that's that's a pretty low terrible  evaluation but basically what you're  deciding is okay our business is worth  this amount of money so if your business  is worth $10,000 that's what we think  our business is worth today  and by you giving us $1,000 then we're  going to give you 10% and how that  person gets paid is when either you do a  dividend from the company to the  shareholders and you know you'll get 90  percent of the dividend and they'll get  10% of the dividend or ultimately the  goal is when you sell the company right  when you sell the company you get bought  for 10 million dollars they get a  million dollars because they own 10% and  you get 9 million dollars so that's the  exit strategy but it's a typically a lot  longer play because you got to build up  the company to get acquired number 3 is  convertible debt with convertible that  it's a mix of the two so this is  favorable for the investors a lot more  than the entrepreneur but typically how  it works is the investor gets a loan  that they're they're getting paid so  you're making them monthly payments on  the loan but at any point the investor  can convert that to equity so if they  feel like you're going to make a big  deal you're going to you're going to get  sold you're going to start paying  dividends then they can say ok you know  what I don't want that anymore I'm going  to convert that into equity so it's a  great way for investors to lower their  risk right there they're getting paid  monthly from you for their debt and then  whenever they see an opportunity to get  to the equity because it's going to be  favorable for them they say okay we're  going to convert our debt and turn it  into equity so not as great for the  entrepreneur but a great deal for  investors and a super common for a lot  of angel investors number four is  royalties and here the investor is not  taking a piece of your company you're  not paying them back a certain fee every  month on a loan but they take a  percentage of every sale that you make  off the top so whatever product you're  selling if it's a hundred dollar product  and they say okay I want a five percent  royalty so that I'm going to give you  the thousand dollars and every time you  make a sale I'm going to take five  percent of that so if you're selling it  for hundred bucks every time you make a  sale you're paying them five dollars but  they don't own any of your business and  you don't own any debt that you have to  pay them back so it's another common  model to use and often they'll still mix  it up they'll say I want debt with a  royalty or I want equity  with the world - I want some debt and  some equity every single deal is  different but those are the four main  components  so just to recap again number one is  debt  number two is equity number three is  convertible debt and number four is  royalty one last thing I want to remind  you guys about is when you're bringing  on an investor you don't just want money  okay you want somebody who can bring  more to the table you want somebody who  can give you advice on how to grow  business you want somebody's going to  open doors and make connections for you  you want somebody who might help you get  into the media or might bring on new  customers for your company you want more  than just the money because you have a  limited amount of people who you can  bring on board right you only have so  much debt you can take on you only have  so much equity you can give away you'll  have so much of a world so you can get  to somebody and you want to make sure  that the people you bring on are  strategic and really want to help you  instead of just giving you money so  always have that caveat in the back your  mind think about this first you bringing  in it's like great I need the money  awesome but what else can they bring to  the table besides the money belief fools  we're watching like the video you can 